module contract;

// CMetrics metric value accessors — resource-projection vocabulary version 1.
//
// Card W37-CORPUS-CONTRACTS-4. TWO TRUSTED ASSUMPTIONS about two upstream
// functions. This module verifies nothing: the C bodies are not analyzed by
// this loader, and a declaration is a premise. Any verdict that rests on one is
// a verdict RELATIVE TO this declaration, never a proof of the C.
//
// Read from the pinned checkout at Fluent Bit
// 66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97 on this card's own run.
//
// ## Why these callees
//
// Each is the SOLE blocker of exactly one projected function in the baseline:
//
//   * `cmt_metric_get_timestamp` -> `check_staled_timestamp`
//     (`lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_encode_prometheus_remote_write.c:732-746`)
//   * `cmt_metric_set_double`    -> `cmt_metric_set`
//     (`lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_metric.c:66-69`)
//
// ## `cmt_metric_get_timestamp`, read from `lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_metric.c:244-251`
//
//     uint64_t cmt_metric_get_timestamp(struct cmt_metric *metric)
//     {
//         uint64_t val;
//
//         val = cmt_atomic_load(&metric->timestamp);
//
//         return val;
//     }
//
//   * `metric` is READ THROUGH and nothing else: one atomic load of one field.
//     It is not freed, not stored, not transferred, and no pointer to it
//     outlives the call. `resource_borrowed` is the faithful mode.
//   * The result is `uint64_t`, a SCALAR. A scalar result carries no custody,
//     so `resource_return_none()` is the faithful — and the only writable —
//     result mode; v1's owning result modes require a pointer return type.
//   * The body dereferences `metric` in its first executable statement with no
//     preceding guard, so `cmt_metric_get_timestamp(NULL)` is upstream
//     undefined behaviour. That is NOT expressible here and is NOT claimed: v1
//     carries a nullability marker only on a RELEASED parameter, and this
//     parameter is borrowed. Recorded so the omission is visible.
//
// ## `cmt_metric_set_double`, read from `lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_metric.c:71-81`
//
//     void cmt_metric_set_double(struct cmt_metric *metric, uint64_t timestamp, double val)
//     {
//         uint64_t tmp;
//
//         tmp = cmt_math_d64_to_uint64(val);
//
//         cmt_atomic_store(&metric->val, tmp);
//         cmt_atomic_store(&metric->val_int64, (uint64_t) ((int64_t) val));
//         cmt_atomic_store(&metric->val_uint64, (uint64_t) val);
//         cmt_atomic_store(&metric->timestamp, timestamp);
//         cmt_atomic_store(&metric->value_type, CMT_METRIC_VALUE_DOUBLE);
//     }
//
//   * `metric` is WRITTEN THROUGH — five atomic stores into five of its
//     fields — and is not freed, not stored elsewhere, not transferred. Writing
//     through a borrowed pointer does not change the caller's custody, which is
//     the same reading `msgpack/packer.qcore` already makes for
//     `msgpack_packer_init`'s output `pk`.
//   * `timestamp` (`uint64_t`) and `val` (`double`) are scalars passed BY
//     VALUE, carrying no custody.
//   * The result is `void` -> `resource_return_none()`.
//   * Every field written is a scalar counter/timestamp/type tag. NO field of
//     `struct cmt_metric` is released, replaced or adopted by this body, so no
//     custody leaves the call in either direction.
//
// ## The duty gate, and why no conflict is expected (constraint 8)
//
// BOTH are defined in `lib/cmetrics/src/cmt_metric.c`, which IS one of the 185
// analysed units (29 projected functions in the baseline). So constraint 8's
// duty gate applies: if W4 derives a summary for either body and that summary's
// custody DUTY contradicts the declaration, the declaration is withdrawn for
// this unit.
//
// No contradiction is expected, and the reason is structural rather than
// hopeful: both declarations are the NEUTRAL ELEMENT — every formal `Borrowed`,
// result `None` — which is the WEAKEST statement the vocabulary can make
// (constraint 4's `N(m)`). A derived summary can only contradict it by claiming
// a formal is released, transferred, delivered or restored, and neither body
// performs any such operation on any parameter. Should a conflict nonetheless
// be reported, it is a `SummaryConflict` diagnostic and the declaration
// governs — never a silent override — exactly as card W36-CORPUS-CONTRACTS-3
// measured for `rd_kafka_mem_free`.
//
// ## ABI (constraint 8)
//
//     lib/cmetrics/include/cmetrics/cmt_metric.h:111
//         uint64_t cmt_metric_get_timestamp(struct cmt_metric *metric);
//     lib/cmetrics/include/cmetrics/cmt_metric.h:96
//         void cmt_metric_set_double(struct cmt_metric *metric, uint64_t timestamp, double val);
//
// One definition of each in the whole tree, external linkage, prototypes
// agreeing with the definitions in types, arity and parameter spelling.
// `struct cmt_metric *` is one object-pointer level, `ptr<mut, u8>`; under the
// LP64 model (`LOADER-ABI-LP64`) `uint64_t` projects to `u64` and `double` to
// `f64`; the `void` result is `unit`.
//
// ## NOT CLAIMED
//
//   * Nothing about atomicity, memory ordering, or whether two threads may call
//     these concurrently. `cmt_atomic_load`/`cmt_atomic_store` have five
//     per-backend definitions in this tree and this projection models none of
//     them.
//   * Nothing about the metric's own lifecycle: who allocated `metric`, who
//     frees it, or whether it is still live. A borrowed declaration says the
//     caller's obligations are UNCHANGED, not that they are discharged.
//   * Nothing about `cmt_math_d64_to_uint64`'s bit-pattern round trip.
//
// ## Read at the same time and DELIBERATELY LEFT UNDECLARED
//
// At W37, `cmt_metric_set` (`:66`), `cmt_metric_set_int64`,
// `cmt_metric_set_timestamp` and the rest of the `cmt_metric_*` family were
// undeclared: `cmt_metric_set` was that card's TARGET rather than its premise.
// W46-METRIC-TRACE-CONTRACT-GAPS later adds the selected scalar helpers below;
// `cmt_metric_set` remains undeclared. `cmt_atomic_load`/`cmt_atomic_store`
// are undeclared because each has five definitions across five backend files
// (`cmt_atomic_clang.c`, `_gcc.c`, `_generic.c`, `_msvc.c` twice), three of them
// `inline` with no `static`/`extern` against a plain extern prototype — the
// `ADD-NAME-COLLISION` hazard, recorded here as `CMT-ATOMIC-BACKEND-COPIES`.
// (Declared at the end of this module by card
// CG10-INDIRECT-ACCESS-SOLE-RESIDUALS, after reading every copy: they agree.)

// --- the metric's timestamp, read atomically ---
extern fn cmt_metric_get_timestamp(metric: ptr<mut, u8>) -> u64
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(metric);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- a double value and its timestamp, stored atomically into the metric ---
extern fn cmt_metric_set_double(metric: ptr<mut, u8>, timestamp: u64, val: f64) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(metric);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(timestamp);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(val);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

